  "body": "**Fixes #716.** Adds an MCP `move_model` tool, so a headless session can place and turn an imported part. Until now, only the GUI's model move could do that.\n\n### What changed\n\n- **`crates/app/src/mcp/model_tools.rs` (new):** the `move_model { name, position: [x, y, z], rotation?: [x, y, z, w] }` tool.\n  - It builds the GUI's own `MoveModel` command and runs it through the document History, so the move is one undo step.\n  - An omitted rotation keeps the part's current one, so lifting a part doesn't untwist it. A non-unit quaternion is normalized by the command, as for fixtures.\n  - The reply holds the part's new placement and a `stock` list: each stock that moved with the part (stock made from it, or a one-part document's stock), with its new placement.\n  - Every refusal is `MoveModel`'s own text, returned as a tool error: a fixture, a work offset, the machine table, an unknown name, a rotation that isn't a quaternion. A short or missing `position` is named too.\n- **`crates/app/src/commands/move_model.rs`:** a new read-only accessor, `MoveModel::following_stock()`. It names the stock the move carries, so the tool can report it.\n- **`crates/app/src/mcp/tools.rs`, `mod.rs`:** the new module's rows are registered after the align tools.\n- **Decision record:** `docs/decisions/716-mcp-move-model.md`.\n\n### Pinning tests (`crates/app/tests/mcp_model.rs`, new)\n\n- `a_moved_part_carries_a_one_part_documents_stock_and_one_undo_puts_both_back`: `tools/list` shows the tool with `name` and `position` required. Dropping a 20 x 10 x 4 box 4 mm moves its bounds to z -4..0. The box stock's bounds move by the same 4 mm and the reply lists that stock. Undo and redo restore and replay both bounds exactly.\n- `a_quarter_turn_swaps_x_and_y_and_a_later_move_without_rotation_keeps_it`: `rotation: [0, 0, 2, 2]` comes back as `[0, 0, 0.7071…, 0.7071…]`, and the part's X and Y extents swap. A later move with no rotation keeps the turn.\n- `move_model_refuses_by_name_and_a_refusal_enters_no_history`: each case is refused by name: a fixture, the work offset, an unknown name, a zero quaternion, a missing position, a 2-number position. None of them moves anything, and the next `undo` still undoes `add_fixture`.\n\nOn `main` all three fail at their first `move_model` call, because the tool doesn't exist there.\n\n### What ran (Windows 10, local)\n\nFork: `austin62jake/Camcraft2`, `main` at upstream f42d3788 (contains v0.16.0, PROCESS_VERSION 4). Gated on 6cec6cca. The only later commit, 964e9d4a, adds a line to the decision file.\n\n- **Quick gate plan** (`scripts/gate.ps1 -Tier quick -DryRun`, run by hand at `-j 6`): no failures, nothing ignored.\n  - App lib: 103 passed.\n  - App suites: `contour_arc_sim` 1, `mcp` 13, `mcp_live` 10, `mcp_model` 3, `mcp_pick` 2, `mcp_pockets` 3, `mcp_render` 5, `mcp_setup` 4, `wall_column_gouge` 3.\n  - UI `models`: 4 passed.\n- **Clippy:** `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings`, clean.\n- **Not run:** Linux, macOS, the batch tier. `camcraft-cli mcp` over stdio wasn't tried either; the tests drive the same `Server::handle` in-process.\n\n### Note for the process files (not changed here)\n\nThe `camcraft-mcp` skill's \"Known Remaining Gaps\" list still has items 2 (setup alignment, now `set_part_axes`) and 6 (pocket recognition, now `detect_pockets`), which have shipped. With this PR, item 1 (`MoveModel`) is done too. I left the skill alone because editing it is a process change: it needs a `PROCESS_VERSION` bump and an announcement on #681.\n\nChangelog: The MCP server can move and rotate an imported part (`move_model`), with the stock that follows it reported back (#716)\n\nFixes #716\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
